[#:seon.task{:name "attribute-selection-reuses-its-immutable-inputs"
             :intent "Retain `seon.schema.datahike/compiled-attribute-selection` for the immutable inputs of `seon.schema/canonical-database-attributes` through `seon.schema/projection-cache-value`. Full and subset form selections remain independent. Repeated selection makes zero calls to `seon.schema.datahike/declared-storable?`, proven through `seon.profile/measure`. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/cost-acquire.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "decoding-pays-a-projection-scope-and-cache-claim-per-value"
             :intent "Each decoded attribute value enters `seon.schema/call-with-projection`, `seon.cache/claim` and compiled-storage separately. An agent's (`seon.db/q` '[:find (pull ?e [*]) :where [?e `:seon.cluster.eval/source` _]] db) over 715 evaluations took 1,529 ms and 937,544 armed calls: projection-cache-value 169,764, call-with-projection 47,309 (756 ms), compiled-storage 47,316 (518 ms), edn-encoded-attr-in? 47,309 (530 ms), `seon.cache/claim` 47,303 (default 2026-10-07). Over a 50 min session compiled-storage ran 551,856 times and allocated 1.85 GB self in one 60 s JFR window; projection-cache-value ran 3.94 M times. Fix: resolve the projection and each attribute's decoder once per query/pull (per result shape), then decode values with the resolved decoders. Done when the 715-entity pull answers in under 150 ms with armed calls proportional to attributes, not datoms; regression: the pull probe as a test with a call-count bound. Read `seon.schema.datahike/compiled-storage`, `seon.schema.datahike/decode-attribute-value-in`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-rank--jfr-large-errors-alloc.txt` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-rank--profile-delta-table.txt`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-attribute-query-calls-logical-attribute-once-per-datom"
  :intent "A whole-database attribute query ([:find ?a (count ?e) :where [?e ?a] [(namespace ?a) ?n] [(contains? ..)]]) over an agent database took 2,153 ms and made 568,597 armed calls, 568,485 of them to `seon.schema.datahike/logical-attribute?` (datahike.clj:919-923 adds it as a predicate clause evaluated for every datom's attribute). Work is proportional to datoms, not to the distinct attributes the answer names. Correct: the predicate (or the attribute-shape check it implements) runs once per distinct attribute, or the clause is planned over the attribute index. Proof: the same query on a fixed database fixture asserts logical-attribute? calls <= distinct attributes, and the evaluation stays under one second. Read `seon.db/q` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/macro-design.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-datahike-fork-collapses-distinct-incomparable-values"
             :intent "Our Datahike fork's safe-compare (reference-code/datahike/src/datahike/datom.cljc:317-321) falls back to class-order, which answers 0 for two distinct values of the same class that compare cannot order, so the index silently keeps one: under :schema-flexibility :read with cardinality-many :t/m, transacting [[:db/add 101 :t/m [1 2]] [:db/add 101 :t/m [1 \"x\"]]] reports two added datoms and stores only [[1 2]]; retracting a stored vector holding a map throws ClassCastException. Seon's write-mode schema type-checks each slot, so no Seon attribute is known to reach it today; any opaque or :db.type/any value would. Fix in the fork at the producer: class-order never answers 0 for distinct values (break ties with a total EDN order, as `seon.schema.datahike/storage-compare` does, or a value hash), or validate-val refuses values compare cannot order. Done when a fork test stores both values and retracts the map-holding vector, and Seon's tests pass on the bumped gitlink. Read `seon.schema.datahike/compiled-storage` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/vectors/vectors.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-slot-refusal-copies-the-whole-stored-value"
             :intent "`seon.schema.datahike/refuse-slot!` puts the whole offending value under `:seon.schema.datahike/value`, so a refused EDN slot carries a copy of the stored text: one refused 300-keyword dependency plan answered an MCP reply of about 3,000 tokens, the whole set printed (default 2026-10-08). Errors hold references, never copies: the refusal names the attribute, the rule and the value's size or digest. Done when a refused slot's error holds no copy of the value and its reply stays small."}]
